WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.067 --> 00:00:03.602 line:0%
We are back with a new episode
of the<i> Wine Down.</i>

00:00:03.703 --> 00:00:06.538 line:0%
And today we are joined
by Kendrick Sampson

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:09.675
who plays Nathan,
to discuss, "Lowkey Trying."

00:00:09.775 --> 00:00:11.711
♪ (UPBEAT MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:15.515 line:0%
So Issa and Nathan
have a big heart-to-heart

00:00:15.615 --> 00:00:17.850 line:0%
this episode.
Do you think Nathan

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:20.586
still has lingering feelings
for Issa?

00:00:20.953 --> 00:00:24.089
-Yeah, yeah.
-(ALL LAUGHING)

00:00:24.623 --> 00:00:28.127 line:0%
I mean, you know, he--
he also has like guilt,

00:00:28.227 --> 00:00:30.429 line:0%
you know?
He went away for a minute

00:00:30.530 --> 00:00:33.032
to figure his shit out
and then all of a sudden

00:00:33.132 --> 00:00:36.536
he's back.
And he's in an intimate space

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:39.138
-with her.
-Is it unreasonable of him

00:00:39.571 --> 00:00:41.874
to expect that
she wouldn't move on?

00:00:41.974 --> 00:00:45.277
Like the reaction to hearing
that she was back with Lawrence

00:00:45.377 --> 00:00:48.280
-was mad shady.
-Nah, I think it's one

00:00:48.381 --> 00:00:49.849
of those things, especially
since he thinks

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:52.285
he has everything together,
where you like, "I'm good,

00:00:52.385 --> 00:00:54.320
you know, whatever happens
happens."

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:56.289
You know, and when
it actually comes face-to-face

00:00:56.389 --> 00:00:58.190
you're like, "Hold up,
you for real?"

00:00:58.290 --> 00:00:59.558
-Yeah.
-"Oh, so you're just gonna

00:00:59.658 --> 00:01:00.559
move on like that?"

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:04.096
But it's also a very dude thing
to put him down immediately.

00:01:04.197 --> 00:01:05.264
-(ALL LAUGHING)
-It's like--

00:01:05.631 --> 00:01:06.766
-KENDRICK: 100 percent.
-"Oh, like the dude that, like,

00:01:06.866 --> 00:01:08.000
ain't got no shoes.
Like, yeah,

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:09.135
well that's who you're with?
Like--"

00:01:09.235 --> 00:01:11.570
You know, even if you're like
over it,

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:13.239
you still gotta be like,
"But I'm better."

00:01:13.339 --> 00:01:15.274
Also, from Nathan's perspective,
you guys have been starting

00:01:15.374 --> 00:01:18.010
to kick it a little bit.
So like in his mind it's like,

00:01:18.110 --> 00:01:19.478
"Okay, I helped you
with the block party,

00:01:19.578 --> 00:01:22.381
I called you afterwards,
you came by my shop,

00:01:22.781 --> 00:01:25.051
we kicked it at the game night."
You know what I mean?

00:01:25.151 --> 00:01:26.386
-So it's like--
-There's some hope.

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:28.521
There's some hope that like,
"Oh, maybe us kicking it

00:01:28.621 --> 00:01:31.357
is like going somewhere."
So it is like a little bit of--

00:01:31.457 --> 00:01:33.026
Like, "I ain't doing this

00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:39.264
for you to reciprocate,
but if you do...

00:01:39.531 --> 00:01:41.634
I ain't gonna be mad at it."
You know?

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:43.035
But it was also like
Nathan was like

00:01:43.569 --> 00:01:45.304
dropping little things, like,
"Well I did give you the idea."

00:01:45.605 --> 00:01:46.739
(ALL LAUGHING)

00:01:46.839 --> 00:01:48.774
For the block party, you know?
He's definitely

00:01:48.874 --> 00:01:50.943
just reminding her, yeah.
Just gently reminding her

00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:53.513
-how dope he was.
-Like... (CHUCKLES)

00:01:54.314 --> 00:01:56.382
"I was just playing,
but for real, you know--

00:01:56.482 --> 00:01:57.916
I did give you the idea."

00:01:58.851 --> 00:02:02.054
How does Issa's perception
of how he ghosted change

00:02:02.154 --> 00:02:04.290
once she learns
the reason why

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:05.858
is he's also bipolar?

00:02:06.225 --> 00:02:09.094
Yeah, I think that
that puts so much

00:02:09.194 --> 00:02:10.329
in perspective.
I think it goes

00:02:10.663 --> 00:02:15.100
from a typical millennial
dating experience

00:02:15.200 --> 00:02:17.636
of a man not being able
to use his words

00:02:17.736 --> 00:02:19.405
and then coming back around,
all of a sudden,

00:02:19.505 --> 00:02:21.073
-to be like, "We good, right?"
-(CHUCKLES) Right.

00:02:21.173 --> 00:02:24.610
To like, "Oh, he actually had
personal mental health issues

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:27.647
that he needed to deal with.
And he needed to take the time

00:02:27.747 --> 00:02:31.017
to do that. You-- You become
more understanding

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.652
because, you know,
we start off this season

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:35.421
in a very self-reflective way,
with Issa being like,

00:02:35.521 --> 00:02:38.190
"Self-care Sunday"
and I think in some ways

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:40.826
that might have been a bit
superficial, and here's,

00:02:40.927 --> 00:02:43.228
you know, someone
who's dealing with something

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.364
where he actually needed
to take care of himself.

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:47.733
-Real self-care.
-Is that superficial?

00:02:47.833 --> 00:02:50.169
I mean self-care Sunday--
Like, we actually do have

00:02:50.269 --> 00:02:51.670
to take care of ourselves,
and sometimes

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.407
that's kind of the debate
that I'm going

00:02:54.507 --> 00:02:56.408
back and forth with.
Like, should she be with him,

00:02:56.508 --> 00:02:59.578
or not? You know, um,
what would I do

00:02:59.679 --> 00:03:01.280
in this situation?
And all that kind of stuff

00:03:01.381 --> 00:03:04.951
is just like, people have
mental health issues

00:03:05.051 --> 00:03:09.322
and it's not necessarily
as scary as a lot of people

00:03:09.422 --> 00:03:12.258
would think it is.
Um, there's a full spectrum

00:03:12.358 --> 00:03:14.994
of-- of mental health issues.
But at the same time,

00:03:15.094 --> 00:03:17.196
we have to factor in
our own health,

00:03:17.296 --> 00:03:20.132
and how we deal with people.
And just realize

00:03:20.232 --> 00:03:21.834
it's just another thing,
you know?

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.303
True.
How do you approach,

00:03:23.403 --> 00:03:24.570
and how does it feel
playing a char--

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:27.139
a Black man, with--
with mental health issues?

00:03:27.239 --> 00:03:29.075
I mean the way I--
I see it,

00:03:29.642 --> 00:03:32.745
you can't grow up in America
and be Black

00:03:32.845 --> 00:03:36.115
and not have some sort
of mental health problems.

00:03:36.215 --> 00:03:38.484
Whether it's PTSD, or something.

00:03:38.584 --> 00:03:41.120
So it was important
for me to play this.

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.188
It was important
for me to have this

00:03:43.288 --> 00:03:46.292
as a discussion,
a conversation starter,

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:48.761
and also to amplify those--
those issues

00:03:48.861 --> 00:03:49.729
that are important to me.

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:51.630
You know you and I
had conversations,

00:03:51.730 --> 00:03:54.167
we had conversations
in the room about, you know,

00:03:54.267 --> 00:03:56.535
how do we reveal this,
how do we do this,

00:03:56.635 --> 00:03:58.838
or does it feel like a...
(IMITATES GLOOMY MUSIC)

00:03:59.338 --> 00:04:00.873
You know what I mean?
As a-- as a thing,

00:04:00.973 --> 00:04:03.242
and how do you honor it,
how we do de-stigmatize it?

00:04:03.343 --> 00:04:05.511
How do we play it as like,
"This is one thing

00:04:05.611 --> 00:04:07.447
I'm dealing with,
but it doesn't, you know,

00:04:07.547 --> 00:04:09.615
define me."
You know what I mean?

00:04:09.715 --> 00:04:11.784
And I believe we did a good job
in terms

00:04:11.884 --> 00:04:14.554
of how it comes out,
and how we talked about it.

00:04:14.921 --> 00:04:16.789
Um, at least on the page,
and I believe

00:04:16.889 --> 00:04:19.125
in the performances as well.
So it doesn't feel

00:04:19.225 --> 00:04:20.960
like we're doing a mic drop
of a thing.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:23.329
That we're just saying, "Hey,
this isn't a thing

00:04:23.429 --> 00:04:24.797
that we need to like
make you just--

00:04:24.897 --> 00:04:27.233
You know, it's an issue,
but it's not, yes,

00:04:27.333 --> 00:04:28.568
this giant plot point
in that way.

00:04:28.668 --> 00:04:30.602
And it's a part of his life
that he's dealing with

00:04:30.703 --> 00:04:32.205
like a lot of us are, you know?

00:04:32.305 --> 00:04:33.306
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:04:33.606 --> 00:04:35.308
It provides some nuance.
And y'all did a good job

00:04:35.408 --> 00:04:36.675
with that, you know...

00:04:36.775 --> 00:04:39.945
-ISSA: Thank you for your help.
-(ALL LAUGHING)

00:04:40.279 --> 00:04:42.448
But-- Well thank you
so much again

00:04:42.548 --> 00:04:44.149
for joining us
for another episode

00:04:44.249 --> 00:04:46.619
of the<i> Wine Down.</i>
Please join us next week

00:04:46.719 --> 00:04:48.754
for the final episode
of the season.

00:04:48.854 --> 00:04:50.756
You don't want to miss it,
see you soon.

00:04:51.156 --> 00:04:52.291
-Cheers. One more.
-Cheers!

00:04:52.391 --> 00:04:53.826
Penultimate!

00:04:53.926 --> 00:04:55.528 line:0%
♪ (MUSIC CONCLUDES) ♪

